{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-03-products-squares-reciprocals",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Products, Squares and Reciprocals of Two Numbers",
    "description": "Use sum-product identities, square relations and reciprocal relations to avoid solving two unknowns the long way.",
    "teachingApproach": "Introduce each identity as a shortcut for a quantity the learner can picture—sum, gap or product—before using symbols. Move through difference from sum/product, product from sum/squares, difference-of-squares, direct product from sum/difference, reciprocal sums, ratio-product scaling and reciprocal quadratics. The capstone reconstructs a sum from product plus sum of squares.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach the identities repeatedly used by the chapter: (x−y)^2=(x+y)^2−4xy, (x+y)^2=x^2+y^2+2xy, x^2−y^2=(x+y)(x−y), and 1/x+1/y=(x+y)/xy. Use them to move between sum, difference, product and sum of squares. Cover ratio-and-product questions by setting the numbers to ak and bk, and single-number reciprocal equations such as x+1/x = constant. Explain why the identity is useful before substituting numbers. Finish with a capstone that reconstructs two numbers from product and sum of squares."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the shortcuts hidden inside two-number puzzles.\n\nIf a question gives a sum, a product or a sum of squares, you often do not need to find both numbers first. The right identity can give the requested quantity directly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose two numbers have sum 30 and product 209. Finding the numbers directly is possible, but the chapter gives a faster route to their difference.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use:\n(x−y)² = (x+y)² − 4xy\n\n= 30² − 4×209\n= 900 − 836\n= 64\n\nSo |x−y| = 8.\n\nThe identity turns two unknowns into one calculation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two numbers have sum 26 and product 165. What is their absolute difference?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
